### `.asgard-cli.yaml` — which platform objects this checkout acts on

`asgard-cli` needs to know which workspace and which pipeline before it can do anything. **Both are
required and neither is derived**, so both are recorded in `.asgard-cli.yaml` beside the
declaration, and that file is committed: a fresh clone and a fresh agent both inherit it.

    asgard-cli workspace show     which one is in effect, and why that one
    asgard-cli workspace use <id> record the workspace — this CLEARS the pipeline line
    asgard-cli pipeline use <id>  record the pipeline

**Nothing is guessed, including from a list of one.** A command with nothing recorded lists the
candidates and refuses to run. The pipeline used to be read off the `origin` remote whenever
exactly one pipeline of the workspace bound it; that is gone, because a repository may have any
number of remotes and which one carries that name is nobody's business but its owner's.

**A pipeline belongs to one workspace, so `workspace use` clears the pipeline line.** Until
`pipeline use` fills it back in, every `asgard-cli pipeline` command here refuses and names the
remedy — which is the failure landing on the next command rather than on whichever later one
happened to be destructive. Commit both lines together. `asgard-cli gate` has a `binding` step
that goes red on the half state.

**One thing this cannot catch: a copy.** Nothing here is checked against a git remote, so a
repository copied wholesale into another repository of the *same* workspace keeps a pipeline id
that still resolves. Run `asgard-cli pipeline use <id>` after copying a repository. Copied into a
different workspace it fails, because the pipeline is not there.

**The platform never reads it.** A run reads the declaration at the pipeline's config path and the
chart it names, and nothing else, so nothing in this file can make a deployment succeed or fail.
`--workspace` and `ASGARD_WORKSPACE` outrank it, which is the safe direction: acting on a test
workspace when the customer's was meant costs a confusing error, and the reverse deploys to a
customer.

**There is no CD workflow in this repository, and no per-environment values files.** The
platform runs the rollout: a tag matching a release's rule produces a run that renders the
chart, checks every CR against the cluster's own CRDs, waits for review and applies. What this
repository holds is the chart and the one declaration.

Adding a project = add `projects/<project>/chart`, declare its releases in
`.asgard-pipeline.yaml`, and create each one on the platform bound to the platform project
whose namespace it deploys into:

    asgard-cli pipeline release create <name> --project <id>

**A project usually needs one release per environment, not one release.** `<project>-dev`
and `<project>-prod` name the same `chart:` directory and differ by `on.pattern`; what
separates them is that each is created against a **different platform project**, and the
platform project is what decides the namespace. One release is the shape for a POC nobody
will maintain — discovering otherwise later means the platform project, the namespace and
the release name are already the ones production is using.

A tag matching a release nobody created produces no run at all — `asgard-cli pipeline
deliveries` says so, and nothing else will. Also add the project to the root `README.md`
table; `asgard-cli check` cross-checks it against the actual folders.

### `.asgard-scaffold.json` — which CLI version shipped the material it owns here

Some of what is in this repository is neither yours nor the platform's: `asgard-cli` ships it.
That is `AGENTS.md` and the design-time skills under `.agents/skills/` that did not come from
`asgard-cli skill update`. This record says **which version of the CLI wrote each of them, and
what it wrote**. It is committed, and nothing edits it by hand.

It is there because **"already present" is not "up to date"**. A newer `asgard-cli` carries newer
skills, and without a record of who wrote the ones here, three states that look identical on disk
cannot be told apart:

    behind   the CLI moved on and nobody here touched the file -> `asgard-cli init --force`
    edited   somebody here changed it, and --force would discard that
    ahead    a NEWER CLI than yours wrote this repo -> upgrade asgard-cli

`asgard-cli init` reports all three, and the third is the one `--force` will not act on: the flag
takes newer shipped material, never older. It also reports a fourth, `retired` — a file some
older CLI shipped here and this one does not, which nothing else in the repository would notice.

**So do not edit `.agents/skills/` by hand.** A correction there is a claim about how this work is
done that the next `--force` replaces; it belongs upstream, in the CLI. The one edit the CLI
expects is the project list at the top of this file, which ships as `TODO` rows for the engagement
to answer — which is why answering one is reported as `edited` and left alone rather than
overwritten.

It is a separate file from `.asgard-docs.json` because that one is the **platform's** record of
the material `asgard-cli skill update` fetches. Two authorities, two records, and the versions in
them are unrelated numbers.

## Entry point shapes — the field-level difference

**Which shape to use is decided by the audience** - see the three decisions
above. This table is what each one looks like once chosen.

| | **A. Agent hub** | **B. Self-hosted workflow, tools inline** | **C. Flow Agent** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Entry | the platform's per-namespace `preset-agent-hub` — you do not author it | your own `BotProvider` → your own `Workflow` | your own `BotProvider` → your own `Workflow` |
| Capabilities bound via | an `Agent` CR; the caller passes `agent_hub.agent_names[]` per turn | `toolsets` / `semanticLayers` written straight onto the `stream-llm-completion-message` processor | `sandboxBlueprint` on that processor → `SandboxBlueprint`'s own `toolsetNames` / `skillSetNames` / `sourceSetMounts` |
| Who can reach it | only a caller that can authenticate | anyone the BotProvider lets in | anyone the BotProvider lets in |
| `Agent` CRs in the chart | one per source system | none | none, unless a blueprint attaches subagents |

Field shapes to keep straight in shape C, all `ValueExprTemplate`: the names
fields carry a **comma-separated string** in `value`; `sourceSetMounts` carries a
**JSON string** the controller unmarshals (`sourceSetName` / optional `subPath` /
`mountPath` starting with `/` / `readOnly`). The workflow's processor **must not
re-declare `toolsets`** — that would be a second declaration of the same
capability, and the two would drift.

**Where a mount may go is not a free choice, and one wrong path takes every
conversation down.** `mountPath` is validated as `^/.+` and nothing else - no
traversal check - and a mount under the agent's `~/.claude` kills the driver at
start-up with a symptom that looks nothing like a mount. `sourceSetMounts` is
also the only mechanism that takes a `subPath`, so it is the only way to isolate
one tenant's files. Read
`.agents/skills/asgard-platform/usecase/conventions.md` before writing one.

## SemanticLayer conventions

Common analysis views go in top-level `sampleQueries[]` (`comment` + `sql`),
**never** as a cube-level `sql:` virtual cube.

**Run every `sampleQuery` against the live database before committing it.** One
that errors or returns nonsense actively misleads the agent that reads it.

**A dimension's `name` is the column its `sql` selects** — `name: ProductId` with
`sql: '{CUBE}.ProductId'`, never a re-cased alias. Measure names are aggregates
rather than columns, so they stay descriptive.

**`description` on every cube, dimension and measure, in 繁體中文** - written
to the rules in `.agents/skills/plain-chinese/`, because a model reads these to
choose what to query and an empty adjective here costs a wrong column rather
than a bad sentence. It is what
the agent reads to decide which column answers a question; one without a
description is effectively invisible to the model.


## This repo is a knowledge base, and you maintain it

Most of what is here is not code. It is what this engagement learned about one
customer's systems, and **this repository is the only place it exists** — the next
agent to open it starts from these files and nothing else. A pile of accurate
documents nobody can navigate is worth about what no documents are worth.

Four layers, and **the tense is the only reason they are separate**:

| layer | tense | answers |
|---|---|---|
| `docs/meeting-notes/` | a moment before it converged | what was actually discussed |
| `docs/decisions/` | a dated snapshot, **immutable** | why it was decided that way |
| `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/` | **always now** | what the system does today |
| `requirements/` | the future, then dead | what will be done |

> **If you read one thing to learn what this system does, read the living spec.**
> `requirements/` is a work queue, not a source of truth: a task spec stops being
> read the moment it reaches `done`, so anything durable left inside one is lost
> in place — still on disk, never opened again.

Three rules follow, and they are the whole discipline:

- **One fact, one home; everywhere else links.** About to copy a paragraph? Link
  instead. Two copies drift, and once they disagree the reader cannot tell which
  is current — so the second copy is worse than none, because it is believed.
- **A change is not done until the bookkeeping is done.** Index updated,
  cross-reference fixed, delta applied to the living spec. That half is yours:
  you do not get bored and can touch many files in one pass. Do it in the
  same pass, rather than reporting that an index needs updating.
- **An answer you had to assemble is new knowledge.** If you answered a question
  by reading a handful of files, the next reader will read the same ones unless you file
  it. Ask: *would they have to redo this?*

`asgard-cli check` audits the mechanical half — pages nothing links to, a module
missing from its index, undated filenames, links that do not resolve. The half
it cannot see (pages that contradict each other, claims a newer source
superseded, concepts nobody owns) is the **`knowledge-base`** skill under
`.agents/skills/`. Run that pass when picking up a repo somebody else worked on,
before a handover, or after a burst of decisions.

Where a fact belongs when it is not obvious:
[`requirements/README.md`](requirements/README.md) for `requirements/`,
[`docs/README.md`](docs/README.md) for the layers,
[`references/README.md`](references/README.md) for the customer's own material.


## Two kinds of skills — design time vs runtime

They are **not interchangeable**. Deciding which one you are writing is the first question,
because the delivery mechanism is completely different.

| | `.agents/skills/<skill>/SKILL.md` | `assets/skills/<skill>/SKILL.md` |
|---|---|---|
| **Who reads it** | the **coding agent** working in this repo (you) | the **deployed Asgard agent**, at runtime |
| **When** | design time — while authoring CRs | runtime — while answering a user |
| **How it is delivered** | read straight from the working tree | committed → `SourceSet` syncs the repo → `SkillSet` `searchPaths` selects it → bound by `Agent.managed.skillSetNames` or `SandboxBlueprint.spec.skillSetNames` |
| **Reaches the cluster?** | never | yes, that is the point |
| **What it can reach** | this laptop - `.env`, and any network path you have | the sandbox, whose environment is a closed list the reconciler builds. **No value this engagement supplies reaches it** |

**The last row is the one that decides a design; the others decide paperwork.**
A runtime skill runs in a real environment, so it can do what a Workflow cannot -
a non-HTTP protocol, a vendor CLI, an API too large to enumerate as tools. What
it cannot do is hold a static service key: there is no `env` on `Agent`,
`SandboxBlueprint` or `SkillSet`, `credentialMounts` resolves only an
`OAuthCredential`, and a hook is an expression in the CR spec. So that route is
open only when the credential **arrives per turn from the caller**, and a system
that needs a static key is reached from a Workflow instead. The fields that
argument was read off are in
`.agents/skills/asgard-platform/usecase/external-api.md`.

**`.agents/skills/db-query/` reads `.env` because it runs on this laptop, not
because it is a skill.** It is the skill you meet first and use most, so it is
the one that teaches by demonstration - and what it demonstrates is true of
design time only. A runtime skill written the same way deploys cleanly and then
tells a person it cannot connect, in a sentence that reads like a missing
setting.

**A SkillSet is a trio, not a CR.** The platform's `POST /v1/skill-set/from-git` creates a
`SkillSet` + its **own** `SourceSet` + the git `Syncer` that fills it, bound 1:1:1. Several
SkillSets sharing one SourceSet leaves the UI unable to find "this skill set's git config", and it
renders as a skill set with no source. Keep one file per SkillSet under
`templates/skill_set/<name>.yaml` holding all three CRs separated by `---`.

Three conventions, and what each one breaks:

| convention | why |
|---|---|
| the SourceSet's only member is **`git/`**, trailing slash included | the reconciler keys directory- vs file-member behaviour off that slash — without it the whole clone is treated as a single file |
| **`asgard-ai.com/managed-by: skill-set`** on the SourceSet *and* the Syncer | it hides the Syncer from the generic list, and it is what the platform cascades on when a SkillSet is deleted |
| a `searchPath` is **one skill directory** | one searchPath resolves to one skill; naming a parent directory silently resolves to nothing. `git/skills/pdf/`, not `git/skills/` |

## Display Annotations

Every CR the Platform UI lists needs an `asgard-ai.com/<kind>-name` annotation, in 繁體中文 —
without it the CR applies cleanly and then appears **nameless** in the UI, which no amount of
linting or dry-running reveals. `asgard-cli verify` enforces it.

These are noun phrases the customer reads in their own console, so the vocabulary
half of `.agents/skills/plain-chinese/` applies: 「訂單查詢」not「智慧化訂單查詢
賦能模組」. The sentence-shape half does not — there is no sentence.

| kind | required | also useful |
|---|---|---|
| `Agent` | `agent-name` | — (`spec.managed.description` already carries the description) |
| `SemanticLayer` | `semantic-layer-name` | — |
| `DataConnector` | `data-connector-name` | — |
| `SkillSet` | `skill-set-name` | `skill-set-description` |
| `SourceSet` | `source-set-name` | `source-set-description` |
| `Syncer` | `syncer-name` | `syncer-description` |
| `BotProvider` | `bot-provider-name` | `additional-annotation` → `embedConfig`(widget 外觀) |
| `SandboxBlueprint` | `sandbox-blueprint-name` | `sandbox-blueprint-description` |
| `Toolset` | `toolset-name` | `toolset-description` |
| `Workflow` | `workflow-name` | — |
| `Trigger` | `trigger-name` | — |
| `KnowledgeBase` | `knowledge-base-name` | — |
| `Loader` | `loader-name` | — |
| `CompletionModel` | `completion-model-name` | — |
| `Plugin` | `plugin-name` | — |

**A SkillSet a Plugin bundles is the one exemption** — it is implementation
detail of the bundle and the platform does not present it, so it carries no
`skill-set-name` and no `managed-by` label. `asgard-cli verify` knows that shape
and does not ask for one.

> **The authority for every platform key is `asgard-ai-platform/asgard-workflow-service`,
> `internal/shared.go`** — every `Annotation*` / `Label*` constant the platform reads, with
> comments on what it does. Read it rather than inferring a convention from another repo's charts.
> **Anything not on `asgard-cli verify`'s list is invisible to every check here**, so a new UI
> label is a check to add, not just a line to write.

## Workflow sets — the metadata that decides whether the UI shows anything

**A `Workflow` CR is not the platform's UI unit; a *workflow set* is** — the group of Workflows
sharing `asgard-ai.com/workflow-set-id`, exactly one of which carries `main-workflow-set: "true"`.
Everything here is UI-only: **nothing changes runtime**, and a CR omitting all of it is perfectly
valid, so `helm lint`, CRD validation and server dry-run all stay green.

| what's missing | what the user sees |
|---|---|
| the set labels | the Workflow is in no set → nothing to list. An empty Flow Agents / Automations page |
| annotation `workflow-set-name` | the set is listed, nameless |
| the Trigger CR's own two labels | the Trigger editor opens as a blank canvas |

| on the Workflow | value | what it decides |
|---|---|---|
| label `workflow-set-id` | the CR name (one workflow = one set is the simple case) | which set this Workflow belongs to |
| label `main-workflow-set` | `"true"` | which member is the set's entry — exactly one. Zero → never listed; two → the front end picks whichever came first |
| label `workflow-key` | the CR name | the Workflow's own identity inside the set |
| label `workflow-set-type` | `bot` / `automation_tool` / `trigger` | which UI section it appears in |
| annotation `workflow-set-name` | 顯示名 | the **set's** name in the UI (`workflow-name` is the single Workflow's) |

**There are three set types, not two.** `trigger` was added later, so a CR dumped from an older
cluster shows `automation_tool` for a Trigger workflow and **is not a template to copy**.

**A `Trigger` needs two labels of its own**, invisible from the Workflow side: its editor reads
`asgard-ai.com/workflow-set-id` (equal to its entrypoint Workflow's) and
`asgard-ai.com/project-environment-id` straight off the Trigger CR. The platform copies both onto
a Trigger it creates itself; **a hand-written CR has nobody to copy them for it.**

**Write only Workflow + Trigger.** The `Trigger` reconciler provisions the BotProvider, its
API-key Secret, and the CronJob itself. Hand-writing a BotProvider for a Trigger is wrong.

## The values the platform injects: `.Values.asgard.*`

**Never write a namespace, a Secret name, an environment id or a version number into a chart.**
The platform layers a reserved block over your values on every run, and the chart reads it:

| key | what it is |
|---|---|
| `.Values.asgard.release` | the helm release name, `iac-<name>` |
| `.Values.asgard.releaseName` | the name as the declaration spells it |
| `.Values.asgard.namespace` | the namespace this release deploys into |
| `.Values.asgard.projectId` | the platform Project id |
| `.Values.asgard.projectEnvironmentId` | that project's main environment id |
| `.Values.asgard.appSecretName` | this release's own Secret |
| `.Values.asgard.appConfigMapName` | this release's own ConfigMap |
| `.Values.asgard.ref` | the tag or branch that triggered the run |
| `.Values.asgard.commitSha` | the full commit SHA |

The chart's `appVersion` is set to the ref for you. A `values.yaml` that defines its own `asgard`
key gets a lint warning and is overridden.

`projectEnvironmentId` surfaces as the label `asgard-ai.com/project-environment-id`, carried by
**`Workflow`, `CompletionModel` and `Trigger`** only. It used to be a value somebody had to fetch
after the namespace existed and paste into a per-environment values file; it is injected now, and
that whole ordering problem is gone.

## When the tool is wrong

`asgard-cli` carries the platform knowledge, and some of it will be wrong or
missing. **That does not get fixed here.** A note in this repository is a note
one engagement has; the next one starts over with the same gap.

    asgard-cli issue-report

It says where to file and what to put in - and the part that gets left out is
worth naming twice: **the state you were in**, so somebody can stand where you
stood, and **where the answer actually turned out to be**, because a page that
does not exist and a page nobody can find need different repairs.

Worth filing even when unsure: "I looked for X and could not tell whether it
exists" is either a missing page or a wrong signpost, and both are fixes.

**Never paste this customer's content into an upstream issue.** Their document,
their systems, their people. The shape is enough.

## Completion Model

**Default: use the platform's builtins by name.** No CR, and no model API key to hold. One
knob — `defaultCompletionModelName`, read by every `SemanticLayer` and overridable per-env.
**Never hardcode a model name in a template**: the builtin tiers are semantic aliases, and a
specific name becomes something to come back and fix when that model is retired.

**A `CompletionModel` CR exists and is the exception, not an impossibility.** Write one when the
customer brings their own model or their own key — three reference deployments do:

    completionModelClass:  aoai-chat | openai-chat | gemini | anthropic | mistral | builtin
    exactly one of:        aoaiChat, openaiChat, gemini, anthropic, mistral, builtin
    the key:               spec.<provider>.apiKey.valueFrom.secretKeyRef into the release Secret

Two things the CRD enforces and helm will not tell you: **`completionModelClass` is immutable** —
changing it means a new CR, not an edit — and exactly one provider block may be present, so a
half-migrated CR with two is rejected by the apiserver, not by `helm lint`.

It also carries `asgard-ai.com/project-environment-id`, like `Workflow` and `Trigger`.

`SemanticLayer.spec.completionModelName` is **required**; an `Agent` takes no model (the caller
picks the orchestrator's model per turn).

## Secrets

**The name is injected, never written.** Each Release has its own Secret and ConfigMap,
`iac-<release>-app-secret` and `iac-<release>-app-config`, created and maintained by the Platform
the moment the Release exists — empty until Variables are set. Their names arrive as
`.Values.asgard.appSecretName` and `.Values.asgard.appConfigMapName`, and the chart reads them
through the chart helper `{{ include "<project>.appSecretName" . }}` — `_helpers.tpl` defines it,
and `asgard-cli add` writes it into every CR that needs a credential.

A chart that writes a name instead points at an object that is not in its namespace, and **nothing
catches it**: the CR is valid, the dry run passes, apply succeeds, `gate` is green, and the
credential fails to resolve at runtime with no error on the CR. If you see a literal `app-secret`
anywhere in a template, that is this bug — the name is real in the Terraform-provisioned demo
namespaces and absent from every Release namespace, which is why it reads as correct.

**A key reaches the Secret only if it is declared.** Two steps, and the first is the one that gets
missed:

    .asgard-pipeline.yaml   appSecret:  - key: <name>      the declaration
    asgard-cli pipeline variables set --release <r> --kind secret <name> --from-file <path>

A value set without a declaration is stored and never injected — `variables list` reports it as
`ORPHAN`, and the run reports `vars/orphan`. `configMapKeyRef` works the same way against
`appConfigMap:`.

**The reverse is quieter and nothing outside `asgard-cli verify` reports it.** A key that is
declared and read by no template is created and injected on every run into a Secret no CR names:
`variables list` shows a value, nothing says `vars/orphan`, and every check is green. It arrives
when a shape changes and whatever read the key is replaced, which leaves a live credential
declared for a system this chart no longer talks to. `verify` warns; the fix is to write the
reader or to delete the declaration, and deciding which is the point of the warning.

Which keys, and how many, is **this deployment's design**, not a rule this file can state:

- **The platform's own resource credential is not one of these.** `SourceSet.apiKey`,
  `Toolset.apiKey` and `BotProvider.apiKey` read `preset-agent-hub` / `api_key`, a Secret the
  platform mints per namespace, and `asgard-cli add` writes that reference directly. **Nothing is
  declared for it and no value has to be obtained** — declaring an `asgard_resource_api_key` of
  your own creates a key nothing reads and a value nobody can supply. Use a declared key only
  where a resource genuinely needs a different credential from the namespace's own, which is a
  requirement — rotation scope and blast radius are the question — so it belongs in
  `requirements/`. The Platform never reads a key's name; it reads whatever `secretKeyRef.key`
  says.
- `asgard-github-pat-password` — only when a git `Syncer` clones a **private** repo.
- one `<system>_db_password` per database. `asgard-cli add dataconnector` prints the exact key
  names it wrote.

Charts **only ever write `secretKeyRef` / `configMapKeyRef`**, never the values, and must **not**
declare the Secret or the ConfigMap themselves — the Platform owns both.

## Deploy

**The platform runs the rollout. There is no CD workflow in this repository and no cluster
credential on any developer's machine.**

A tag matching a release's rule in `.asgard-pipeline.yaml` produces a **run**, which walks six
steps: Checkout, Lint, Variables, Render & Dry-run, Review, Apply. The dry run sends every
rendered CR to the apiserver, so the CRDs' own CEL rules, patterns and required fields are checked
against the real cluster before anything is written. Apply then writes this release's Secret and
ConfigMap, does `helm upgrade --install`, and triggers the Syncers marked
`asgard-ai.com/auto-fire-on-rollout: "true"`.

    git push origin dev-0.1.0
    asgard-cli pipeline runs watch --release <name> --ref dev-0.1.0

`watch` waits for the run the push created, follows the steps and prints the plan report or the
reason it failed. A run stops at review unless the release has auto-apply on:

    asgard-cli pipeline runs approve <run-id>

**Auto-apply belongs to the release, not to a run**, and it is not fixed at create time:

    asgard-cli pipeline release update <name> --auto-apply
    asgard-cli pipeline release update <name> --no-auto-apply

It is read when a plan finishes, so a run already waiting for review keeps waiting either way.

**Apply uses the chart and values captured when the plan ran**, not the repository as it is now,
so a tag moved or a file edited since then changes nothing about a run waiting for review.

> **The ordering trap that remains:** a tag matching a release **nobody created on the platform**
> produces no run at all. `asgard-cli pipeline deliveries` says so, and nothing else will.
> Create it once, bound to the project whose namespace it deploys into:
>
>     asgard-cli pipeline release create <name> --project <id>

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Do not `helm upgrade` from a laptop**, and there is nothing to upgrade with — no cluster
> credential is issued to a client. A Syncer that pins `revision: {{ .Chart.AppVersion }}` needs
> the ref the platform stamps into `appVersion`; a local install would render a placeholder as a
> git ref that does not exist and the Syncer would fail to clone every run. `asgard-cli render`
> renders only, with placeholder values, and has no install path.

## Helm Commands

```bash
# Render a release locally. The chart comes from its entry in .asgard-pipeline.yaml and the
# reserved .Values.asgard.* block is filled with placeholders.
asgard-cli render <release>
```

**What renders here is not what deploys.** The platform renders with the release's real values and
real ids and then checks every CR against the cluster's CRDs; this renders with placeholders and
checks nothing. It is for the loop that is too fast to involve a push — does the template compile,
does it produce the objects I meant. The authoritative answer is the plan report.

`asgard-cli render` needs only `helm` on PATH.

**There is no `helm lint` line here, and that is deliberate.** Linting a chart on its own defaults
is what catches a `.Values.*` with no default, and it is `asgard-cli gate`'s `lint` step — which
supplies the reserved `asgard` block and nothing else. A bare `helm lint <chart>` supplies nothing,
so it fails on every chart that reads `.Values.asgard.projectEnvironmentId`, which is every chart
that labels anything. **That failure looks like the chart is broken and it is not.** The one step
of the gate you cannot run alone is also the one whose obvious substitute is wrong, so run the gate.

## Acceptance Gate

**This gate is the chart's. A deck has its own, and no command runs it.**
`asgard-cli gate` reads CRs, so nothing here looks at a slide — and the failure a
deck actually has is a sentence, not a field. Before any deck leaves this
repository, run `## Before you send it` in `.agents/skills/proposal-deck/SKILL.md`,
**starting at question 0**, and the second pass in
`.agents/skills/plain-chinese/SKILL.md`, which asks two questions rather than
one. Both are per-sentence rather than per-file, and both exist because the same
correction was made seven times on one draft by a person reading it.

Load the **`asgard-cr-verification`** skill (`.agents/skills/`) and run its gate; release omitted =
every release. **Any red step stops the work.**

That skill comes from the platform rather than from the CLI, so it describes the Asgard server
this repository deploys to. If it is not there, or the CLI says the material here is behind:

```bash
asgard-cli skill update    # writes it, plus the CR shapes and the processor catalogue
```

Locally, before pushing, **one command**:

```bash
asgard-cli gate            # every release
asgard-cli gate <release>  # one of them
```

It runs everything this machine can check, in order, and a step that could not run is reported
as skipped rather than passed:

**`asgard-cli gate --help` is the authoritative list**; the table below is a convenience copy in
this repository and moves only when `asgard-cli init` is re-run — which is exactly what the
`shipped` step below reports, and this table is one of the files it reports on.

| step | what it proves |
|---|---|
| `tools` | `helm` is on PATH; without it the chart steps cannot run |
| `repo` | the structural invariants a chart render cannot see |
| `shipped` | this file and the design-time skills are still what the installed `asgard-cli` carries. **The one freshness check that always runs** — it compares against the binary rather than a platform, so no session and no `--offline` skips it. `edited` and `ahead` warn rather than fail: the first is somebody here having changed shipped material on purpose, the second is the binary being older than this repository, which is an install to upgrade rather than a repository to fix |
| `binding` | `.asgard-cli.yaml` names a workspace and a pipeline the platform still has. Catches a half-bound checkout — `workspace use` clears the pipeline line — before some later command acts on it |
| `skills` | the reference material here still describes the server this repo deploys to |
| `lint` | `values.yaml` defaults every `.Values.*` the chart itself owns. It supplies the reserved `asgard` block and nothing else — linting with no `-f` at all fails on every chart that reads `.Values.asgard.*`, which a chart must not declare |
| `render` | each release renders |
| `verify` | the rendered CRs against each other: dangling references, both halves of every entrypoint, display annotations, workflow-set labels, the agent-split invariants |

The individual commands (`asgard-cli check`, `asgard-cli verify`) still exist and are what to
reach for when one step fails and you want only that one.

Then push, because the rest cannot be done here:

4. `asgard-cli pipeline runs watch --release <name> --ref <tag>`

**Step 4 is not a formality and it is not skippable.** It is where every rendered CR is sent to
the apiserver with a server-side dry run, so the CRDs' CEL rules, patterns, required fields and
unknown-field pruning are checked against the real cluster. Nothing local can do that: no cluster
credential is issued to a client, which is the whole reason steps 1–3 are the shape they are —
they check what a dry run passes and runtime still fails.

5. `asgard-cli pipeline manifest --release <name> --summary` — what is on the cluster now.

**Step 5 exists because step 4 does not always prove anything.** The rollout's apply step fires
the Syncers this release deploys that carry `asgard-ai.com/auto-fire-on-rollout` and waits for
them, so a release **with no Syncer** has nothing after the dry run that proves the platform
accepted any of it — a succeeded run means helm returned. `asgard-cli verify` warns about exactly
that, and this is what to run when it does. Add `--status` for what each object reports about
itself.

**Read what it can and cannot tell you.** `ok` is derived from presence, and roughly half the
Asgard kinds declare no status schema at all — a chart of a `DataConnector` and a `SemanticLayer`
is one where no object can report anything, so presence really is all there is and the only
verification left is to open the product and ask. Knowing that in one command beats discovering it
by dumping YAML.

**A Kubernetes Event is where a reconciler's complaint lands when the CR has nowhere to put it, and
no command here will ever show you one.** That is deliberate: a release's deploy identity is scoped
to `asgard-ai.com` so that it can only read back what it was allowed to create, and events are a
core-group kind it never writes. Widening that identity for every release, permanently, to serve a
diagnostic is not a trade this platform makes. Where a CR reports nothing, the verification is to
open the product and use the thing.

`pipeline manifest` reads live state and does not diff: comparing the cluster against the IaC
source needs the source, and the source is here. Render locally and compare — live against the
render of the deployed commit says somebody changed the cluster; the render of HEAD against the
deployed commit says the repository is ahead.

Two more `pipeline` subcommands are worth knowing and are not named above:
`asgard-cli pipeline repos --connection <id>` lists what a provider connection can reach (a
repository missing from it was not granted to the installation, which is changed on the provider),
and `asgard-cli pipeline releases` lists the releases the platform holds for this pipeline.

**What the plan found, and what each rule code means, is the platform's document, not this one.**
`.agents/skills/asgard-cr-verification/` is fetched from the server this repository deploys to and
rewritten on every `asgard-cli skill update`; it carries the run steps and every rule
code. It says nothing about `asgard-cli gate`, deliberately — a server cannot know which version
of the CLI is installed here. Local half: `asgard-cli gate --help`. Server half: that skill.

## Spec-Driven Development

- Treat `requirements/` as implementation source of truth; `references/` is background only.
- Task specs: `requirements/tasks/TASK-xxx-*.md`, registered in `requirements/tasks/_index.md`.
- Status flow: `draft` → `ready` → `in-progress` → `done` (never `in_progress`).
- **What may live in `requirements/` at all is ruled by
  [`requirements/README.md`](requirements/README.md)** — two record types and
  nothing else, and the tense test that decides the rest: `requirements/` is
  written in the future tense, the living spec in the present. A sentence still
  true after the work is finished belongs in the living spec, not in a task spec
  that stops being read at `done`. Read it before putting anything in
  `requirements/` that is not a `REQ-*.md` or a `TASK-*.md`.
- SDD is **required** for a new `SemanticLayer`/`DataConnector`, widening an agent's queryable
  cubes, or re-introducing a write path — the databases are real.

**This file and the living spec answer different questions, and a fact belongs to exactly one:**

| | `AGENTS.md` (here) | `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/` |
|---|---|---|
| Answers | **how to change this repo** | **what the system does** |
| Holds | layout, template conventions, Helm/CRD gotchas, how to run the gate | capability boundaries, data 口徑, routing rules, state machines, the security argument |
| Test | "not knowing this breaks the chart" | "not knowing this makes the behaviour wrong" |

**When a change lands**, two obligations beyond the code: apply the behaviour delta to the matching
`docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/<module>.md`, and write one `docs/decisions/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md` for
each decision that got settled.

## Helm / Platform Gotchas

- All resources use `apiVersion: asgard-ai.com/v1alpha1`.
- **Differences between releases are values, and values live on the platform.** Two releases of one
  chart differ by what `asgard-cli pipeline variables set` put on each; the chart has one
  `values.yaml`, and it holds defaults.
- **A field the CRD does not declare is silently pruned, and a plain `kubectl apply
  --dry-run=server` calls that success.** Helm's server-side apply does not — it builds a typed
  patch and fails with `field not declared in schema`. The platform's plan checks for this and
  reports it as `crd/unknown-field` before anything is applied, which is why the plan report is
  the step that cannot be skipped.
- **Read CRD shapes from the cluster you deploy to**, not from a local checkout of the CRD repo —
  the two can disagree, and a deprecated field is easy to find in an older checkout.
- **`Workflow.spec.processors[].configs[].name` is a free-form string.** A wrong config name lints
  clean, passes CRD validation, and then does nothing at runtime. A typo in a *reference* is
  caught by `asgard-cli verify`; an invented *key* is not.
- **A failed `helm upgrade` can leave the namespace partially applied.** The previous revision
  stays `deployed`, so the next upgrade proceeds without a rollback — but do not assume "the deploy
  failed" means "nothing changed".
- **A resource the platform created cannot be taken over by helm without a one-time adoption.**
  Helm refuses with `invalid ownership metadata` unless the live object already carries
  `app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm`.
- `Toolset.tools[]` require `requestConsent` (bool); `Agent.managed.prompt` requires all of
  `persona`/`task`/`context`/`format`, and `managed.aliasName` + `managed.description` are required.
- Render with `asgard-cli render` before committing template changes; there is no app to run.

